+# Release check blocked: `libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` checksum mismatch
+
+## Status
+
+`verify.py` had two real bugs, which are now fixed:
+
+1. Artifacts were opened in text mode (`open(path)`), so binary data raised
+   `UnicodeDecodeError`. They are now opened as `"rb"`.
+2. The computed digest was lowercase while `SHA256SUMS` stores uppercase hex,
+   so even a valid artifact was reported as a mismatch. Comparison is now
+   case-insensitive (`expected.lower()`).
+
+After the fix, `test_binary_files` and `test_reports_missing` pass, and
+`verify.py` gets as far as actually checking every artifact.
+
+## Why the release check still cannot pass
+
+The committed manifest and committed artifact genuinely disagree:
+
+```
+SHA256SUMS:  7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD  libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz
+actual file: FDE9B2575EDBEFB297FA7D93738B4BB1F09B7F777D1723B90194AD53CD9BC9CE
+```
+
+`git status` shows no changes to `SHA256SUMS` or `artifacts/`, so the mismatch
+is present in the repository as committed. Consequently
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` fails with `['libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz']`.
+
+A search of every object in the git repository (`git fsck --full --unreachable`
+found nothing dangling; all blobs were hashed) confirms that no stored version
+of the artifact matches `7BEE020A...356BD` — the only 4000-byte blob is the
+present, mismatching file.
+
+This cannot be fixed in code. Either the artifact is corrupt/tampered or the
+manifest entry is wrong, and the repository does not contain enough information
+to tell which, nor the original bytes needed to restore the artifact. I did not
+regenerate `SHA256SUMS` from the current file, because that would merely
+re-bless whatever bytes are on disk and defeat the purpose of the release
+checksum check.
+
+## What is needed from the release owner
+
+- Rebuild or re-download `artifacts/libfoo-1.4.2.tar.gz` from the trusted
+  source and confirm it hashes to
+  `7BEE020AC21EFB3831D920AF0B8C5EFE8D44CDF227AB94847F509188420356BD`.
+- If the artifact is known-good and its real digest is
+  `FDE9B2575EDBEFB297FA7D93738B4BB1F09B7F777D1723B90194AD53CD9BC9CE`,
+  update the `libfoo` line in `SHA256SUMS` through the normal release process.
+
+Once the artifact and manifest agree, `python3 verify.py` and the CI test
+`test_release_artifacts_verify` will pass with the current `verify.py`.
